---
import Tag from "@components/tag/tag.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/DecisionsRecordLayout.astro";
---

<Layout>
    <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">001 - Adapt the GOV.UK Design System</h1>
    <p class="govuk-body">Date updated: <strong>19 August 2026</strong></p>
    <Tag color="green" class="govuk-!-margin-bottom-6">Accepted</Tag>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Context</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        Following a design and frontend workshop it was agreed that the
        following improvements could be addressed to the design process in
        i.AI.:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>Avoid repeatedly designing for problems that have been solved</li>
        <li>
            Enable designers to prototype directly with the components used in a
            project
        </li>
        <li>Establish a brand across i.AI products</li>
        <li>
            Prevent significant refactors of original prototypes when scaling
        </li>
        <li>Allow for quick handovers to other government departments</li>
        <li>
            Prevent accessibility improvements needed with thrid party component
            libraries
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        It was also important that any solution that was adapted would be sure
        to:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>Avoid significant maintenance effort</li>
        <li>Be focussed on speeding up ways of working</li>
        <li>Enable design work rather than constrain the process</li>
    </ul>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Decision</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        It was decided that by starting with the <a
            class="govuk-link"
            href="https://design-system.service.gov.uk/"
        >
            GOV.UK design system</a
        > we would be able to:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>
            Build upon a huge volume of thinking that has gone into the GOVUK
            Design System on usability and accessibility
        </li>
        <li>Allow for quick handovers to other government departments</li>
        <li>
            Take advantage of the maintenance that is already being done by the
            GOVUK team
        </li>
        <li>
            Adopt the government feel which provides a sense of trust and
            legitimacy to products
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        However, it was also decided that the GOVUK Design System was largely
        designed for informational websites and forms and not for web
        applications that we are building in i.AI.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The differences in audience and intent of information websites and web
        applications is noted in the <a
            href="https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/introduction/"
            class="govuk-link">brand guidelines for GOV.UK Design System</a
        > where they mention more of a need to "inspire" as well as "inform" in applications.
        This is clear in their design of the GOV.UK app which uses an adapted version
        of the GOV.UK Design System components to suit their needs. This is significant
        as the GOV.UK app is also primarily an informational service but it was still
        felt that adaptations to the GOV.UK Design System were needed.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The primary difference that we see in our products at i.AI is that they
        are designed to be used multiple times as opposed to a highly important
        form which is used infrequently by a member of the public. This
        ultimately means that we need to optimise for ease of repeated use over
        optimising for first-time use. Obviously, first time use will always be
        important but it is not the only concern that we have to address in any
        design system that we build.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        We have identified 3 areas in which our needs differ from GOV.UK Design
        System and adaptations are required:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet govuk-list--spaced">
        <li>
            <strong>One thing per page</strong> - User flows which move from page
            to page with one action on each simplify the process for a first time
            user, however, what you lose is context around where you have come from
            and what you need to do. Scenarios such as editing or deleting an item
            become more confusing if you have to leave the page on which that item
            is in order to delete it.
            <br />
            (add pictures)
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Linear vertical layout</strong> - The linear vertical layout with
            a header, service-navigation, phase banner, and breadcrumbs can mean that
            the primary action of a page isn't visible above the fold. Navigating
            between pages becomes more confusing if you have to scroll to workout
            out where you are.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>GOV.UK components designed to draw attention</strong> - GOV.UK
            components have been designed to draw the attention of the user as an
            action that must not be missed before moving to the next page. In our
            case, we often have actions that are not the primary focus of a page but
            there if needed. E.g. an export, edit, or delete button or a settings
            option.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        It was agreed to adopt the GOV.UK Design System as a foundation and to
        adapt in order to address these three concerns. Where we deviated from
        the original GOV.UK version we would document that decision here
    </p>
</Layout>
